/// Chapter 15 of the guide, written from md-docs/15-styling.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page StylingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/styling", title: "Styling", description: "Themes and tokens, style blocks with splices, nesting and media queries, transitions, classes and stylesheets, dark mode, responsive layout.", layout: DocsShell("Styling", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "15" }), blurb: "Every built-in ships styled; a theme changes the whole site; a style block is real CSS scoped to its element.", source: "md-docs/15-styling.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "design-tokens-and-the-theme", label: "Design tokens and the theme"), Anchor(id: "style", label: "style \{ \}"), Anchor(id: "transition", label: "transition \{ \}"), Anchor(id: "classes-and-your-own-stylesheet", label: "Classes and your own stylesheet"), Anchor(id: "when-your-rule-and-a-built-in-s-disagree", label: "When your rule and a built-in's disagree"), Anchor(id: "enum-props-as-hooks", label: "Enum props as hooks"), Anchor(id: "dark-mode", label: "Dark mode"), Anchor(id: "layout-without-css", label: "Layout without CSS"), Anchor(id: "responsive-values", label: "Responsive values"), Anchor(id: "what-the-build-emits", label: "What the build emits")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/expressions", prevLabel: "14. {t("ch.14")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/forms", nextLabel: "16. {t("ch.16")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("Every built-in ships styled, and a theme changes the whole site by changing its tokens. When you need more, an element takes a `style \{ \}` block of real CSS, scoped to that element, with tokens and live values spliced in.")

        Heading("Design tokens and the theme", id: "design-tokens-and-the-theme").h2

        Markdown("A theme is a set of tokens, each of which becomes a CSS custom property (`--color-primary`). A `theme` declaration overrides what the baseline carries; every token not listed keeps its baseline value:")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Brand {
    color-primary: #0F766E
    color-secondary: #134E4A
    color-background: #FFFFFF
    color-surface: #F8FAFC
    color-text: #0F172A
    color-text-muted: #64748B
    color-border: #E2E8F0
    font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif
    font-family-mono: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace
    radius-md: 14px
    spacing-lg: 2.5rem
    shadow-md: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.08)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A project with one theme uses it; with several, `webfluent.app.json` chooses: `\"theme\": \{ \"name\": \"Brand\" \}`. Values are raw CSS to the end of the line.\n\nThe baseline carries 71 tokens in groups — colour, font, spacing, radius, shadow, motion, easing, breakpoints, code and layout — every one a CSS custom property on `:root`, readable from a style block as `$name` and from a stylesheet as `var(--name)`. [Design tokens](/docs/guide/tokens) lists each with its baseline value. Moving a breakpoint moves every media query the build writes with it.\n\nA theme may add tokens of its own (`surface-raised: #131519`); they become custom properties like the rest. The contrast lint (`A13`) checks text against background pairs in the theme and warns below WCAG AA.\n\n`theme.builtin` in `webfluent.app.json`: `full` (default) ships the baseline look of every built-in you use; `structural` ships only their layout and mechanics — focus rings, overlays, the grid — for a site that brings its whole design.")

        Heading("style \{ \}", id: "style").h2

        CodeBlock(##"page Styled(path: "/") {
    state accent = "#D97706"
    state wide = false
    Card {
        style {
            padding: $lg                       // a token, resolved by the property: spacing-lg
            background: $surface               // color-surface
            border: 1px solid $border
            border-radius: $radius-lg
            box-shadow: $shadow-md
            border-left: 4px solid {accent}    // a live value: repaints when `accent` changes
            max-width: {if wide { "100%" } else { "40rem" }}
            &:hover { box-shadow: $shadow-lg }
            & > p { margin: 0 }
            @media (max-width: 640px) { padding: $sm }
        }
        Text("Styled card")
        Button("Widen") { on click { wide = !wide } }
    }
}"##, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- A style block holds CSS declarations, one per line or `;`-separated. No quotes around values — `padding: 2rem`, not `\"2rem\"`.\n- `$token` names a token. A short name is resolved by the property: `padding: $lg` is `spacing-lg`, `color: $primary` is `color-primary`, `border-radius: $lg` is `radius-lg`, `box-shadow: $md` is `shadow-md`, `font-size: $xl` is `font-size-xl`, `transition: $fast`. A full name (`$color-primary`, `$surface-raised`) works anywhere. Check a token's spelling: one no theme defines resolves to nothing, and the browser drops the declaration.\n- `\{expr\}` splices a live value; the declaration updates when the value changes. A whole value or a part of one (`\{n\}px`).\n- `&:hover`, `&:focus-visible`, `&.active`, `& > p`, `&[aria-current]` nest as CSS nesting does; `@media (…) \{ \}` nests too.\n- The block is scoped: it becomes a class on that element alone, written to the page's stylesheet at build time. Splices become custom properties set by the runtime.\n\nThe static parts of every `style \{ \}` are compiled to CSS; the linter warns when a declaration is invalid CSS.")

        Heading("transition \{ \}", id: "transition").h2

        CodeBlock(#"page Fade(path: "/") {
    state on = false
    Card {
        style { opacity: {if on { 1 } else { 0.4 }}; transform: {if on { "none" } else { "scale(0.98)" }} }
        transition { opacity: $fast; transform: 200ms ease-out }
        Text("Fades")
    }
    Button("Toggle") { on click { on = !on } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`transition \{ prop: duration easing \}` sets CSS transitions for the element; a `$fast/$normal/$slow` token or a raw value. Reduced-motion readers get none ([chapter 20](/docs/guide/motion)).")

        Heading("Classes and your own stylesheet", id: "classes-and-your-own-stylesheet").h2

        Markdown("`class: \"name\"` adds a class from your own stylesheet; a `.css` file in `src/` is bundled into `styles.css`, and `meta.stylesheets` in `webfluent.app.json` links external ones.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Classes(path: "/") {
    state active = true
    Container(class: "hero") {
        Heading("Welcome", class: if active { "title is-on" } else { "title" }).h1
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The value may be an expression, so a class can follow state. Built-ins wear `wf-*` classes (`wf-btn`, `wf-btn--primary`, `wf-card`), stable across releases, so a stylesheet may target them.")

        Heading("When your rule and a built-in's disagree", id: "when-your-rule-and-a-built-in-s-disagree").h2

        Markdown("Three layers of CSS meet on a page, in this order of strength:\n\n1. **The built-ins' own rules** (`.wf-btn`, `.wf-card`), shipped in `styles.css`.\n2. **Your stylesheets** (`src/**/*.css`), bundled after them — so on equal specificity, yours win.\n3. **`style \{ \}` blocks**, compiled to rules with tripled specificity, and nested state and media rules marked `!important` — so an element's own block beats any sheet, as the inline style it replaces would.\n\nSo: to restyle one element, use its `style \{ \}`. To restyle every button, write `.wf-btn \{ … \}` in a stylesheet, or change the tokens in a theme — the built-ins read tokens, so `color-primary` recolours every primary button, link and focus ring at once. `theme.builtin: \"structural\"` drops the built-ins' look entirely and keeps only their layout and mechanics, for a project that brings its whole design.")

        Heading("Enum props as hooks", id: "enum-props-as-hooks").h2

        Markdown("A component's enum props are written to its root element as `data-<prop>`, so one stylesheet rule styles every variant ([chapter 11](/docs/guide/components#enum-props)):")

        CodeBlock(#".note[data-tone="loud"] { font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-danger) }"#, title: "css")

        Heading("Dark mode", id: "dark-mode").h2

        Markdown("A second `theme` declaration named in `webfluent.app.json` as `\"theme\": \{ \"name\": \"Light\", \"dark\": \"Night\" \}` stands in for dark mode. Its tokens override the light theme's under the reader's system preference (`prefers-color-scheme: dark`) and whenever `setTheme(\"dark\")` was called; `setTheme(\"light\")` forces light, `setTheme(\"system\")` follows the system again. The choice is remembered across visits and applied before the first paint, so a dark reader never sees a light flash.")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Light {
    color-background: #FFFFFF
    color-text: #0F172A
}

theme Night {
    color-background: #0B1220
    color-surface: #111827
    color-text: #E5E7EB
    color-text-muted: #9CA3AF
    color-border: #1F2937
}

page Prefs(path: "/") {
    Text("You are on the {theme} theme")
    Row(gap: .sm) {
        Button("Light") { on click { setTheme("light") } }
        Button("Dark") { on click { setTheme("dark") } }
        Button("System") { on click { setTheme("system") } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Everything that uses tokens — the built-ins, your `$token` styles — switches with it; a color written raw does not, so prefer tokens. The `theme` value is `\"light\"`, `\"dark\"` or `\"system\"`.")

        Heading("Layout without CSS", id: "layout-without-css").h2

        Markdown("Most layout needs no `style` at all: `Stack`, `Row`, `Grid`, `Container`, `Spacer` and `Divider` with their `gap:`, `align:`, `justify:` and `columns:` props ([chapter 7](/docs/guide/elements#layout-elements)).")

        CodeBlock(#"page Layout(path: "/", title: "Layout", description: "A grid of projects.") {
    Container {
        Stack(gap: .lg) {
            Row(justify: .between, align: .center) {
                Heading("Projects").h2
                Button("New").primary.sm
            }
            Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 2, lg: 3 }, gap: .md) {
                for n in 1..=6 { Card { Text("Project {n}") } }
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Responsive values", id: "responsive-values").h2

        Markdown("Any layout prop takes **one value per breakpoint**:")

        CodeBlock(#"Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 2, lg: 3 }, gap: { base: .sm, lg: .lg }) { … }
Row(direction: { base: .column, md: .row }) { … }
Column(span: { base: 12, md: 6 }) { … }"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("That compiles to one class and one media query per step — no JavaScript, no resize listener, and **the right layout in the very first paint**, where a `viewport` test could only paint one branch and swap after hydration.\n\nThe steps are `base`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`, `xl`, and each is a design token — `screen-sm` … `screen-xl`. Move one in your theme and every media query moves with it, including `viewport.md`:")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Brand { screen-md: 900px }"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A step a value omits keeps whatever the step below it said, so `\{ base: 1, lg: 3 \}` is one column until `lg`.\n\nThe same names are sugar inside a `style \{ \}` block:")

        CodeBlock(#"Heading("Title").h1 {
    style {
        font-size: 24px
        @md { font-size: 34px }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("A layout stays as it is written", id: "a-layout-stays-as-it-is-written").h3

        Markdown("The engine used to reflow **every** `Row` into a column and **every** `Grid` into one column below 768px, with `!important`. It does not any more. A layout that should reflow says so:")

        CodeBlock(#"Row.stacks { … }                          // a column on a narrow screen
Grid.stacks { … }                         // one column on a narrow screen
Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 3 }) { … }   // or say what it does at each width"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`wf migrate` adds `.stacks` to every `Row`, `Grid` and `Column` of a WebFluent 2 project, so the old behaviour carries over — written down, where you can see it and change it.\n\n`viewport` ([chapter 8](/docs/guide/state#the-browser-as-values)) stays for what is genuinely behavioural — a drawer instead of a sidebar — and follows `matchMedia`, which fires once when the answer changes rather than on every pixel of a drag.")

        Heading("What the build emits", id: "what-the-build-emits").h2

        Markdown("`styles.css` holds the tokens something in the output uses (`:root \{ --color-primary: … \}`), the dark overrides, the CSS of the built-ins you used (and only those), your bundled stylesheets, and the compiled `style \{ \}` rules that more than one page reaches. A rule only one page can reach goes to `pages/<Name>.css`, loaded with that page. [Performance](/docs/guide/performance) shows how to see what each weighs.")
    }
}
